# `ode::OdeMethod`

The step rule of `fluxa`.

`RK4` is the classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta rule with a fixed number of equal steps (`OdeOptions::steps`): four evaluations per step, error of order `h⁴`, a cost known in advance. `RK45` is the adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4) pair: it adjusts the step to a requested tolerance, which is cheaper when the solution is smooth in places and needs fine steps in others. The adjoint solver [`fluxa_adjoint`](fluxa_adjoint.md) and the event solver [`fluxa_until`](fluxa_until.md) are defined for `RK4` only.

## Fields

### `RK4`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

Fixed-step classical Runge-Kutta.

### `RK45`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

Adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4).

## Guidance

Set as `OdeOptions::method`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Both methods on `y' = −y`. RK4 uses a fixed 400 evaluations; RK45 uses what the tolerance requires.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace ode = pluss::subsp::math::ode;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(12));
  const ode::OdeFn decay = [](double, const Series &y, const Series &) { return y * -1.0; };   // y' = -y, exact solution e^{-t}

  for (const ode::OdeMethod method : {ode::OdeMethod::RK4, ode::OdeMethod::RK45}) {
    ode::OdeOptions options;
    options.method = method;
    const ode::OdeResult r = ode::fluxa(decay, pluss::make_series({1.0}), 0.0, 1.0, pluss::series::undefined(), options);
    out.writeln(static_cast<int>(method), ": error =", std::abs(r.y_final.items<double>()[0] - std::exp(-1.0)), ", evaluations =", r.function_evaluations);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

A fixed method ignores tolerances; an adaptive one honours them. Tightening the tolerance buys accuracy with more steps.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace ode = pluss::subsp::math::ode;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(14));
  const ode::OdeFn decay = [](double, const Series &y, const Series &) { return y * -1.0; };   // y' = -y, exact solution e^{-t}

  for (const double tol : {1e-4, 1e-8, 1e-12}) {
    ode::OdeOptions options;
    options.method = ode::OdeMethod::RK45;
    options.relative_tolerance = tol;
    options.absolute_tolerance = tol * 1e-3;
    const ode::OdeResult r = ode::fluxa(decay, pluss::make_series({1.0}), 0.0, 1.0, pluss::series::undefined(), options);
    out.writeln("tolerance", tol, ": steps =", r.steps, ", error =", std::abs(r.y_final.items<double>()[0] - std::exp(-1.0)));
  }
}
```

### Example 3

Adjoint and event solving need `RK4`: asking for another method is rejected.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace ode = pluss::subsp::math::ode;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const ode::OdeFn decay = [](double, const Series &y, const Series &) { return y * -1.0; };
  ode::OdeOptions options;
  options.method = ode::OdeMethod::RK45;
  try {
    (void)ode::fluxa_adjoint(decay, pluss::make_series({1.0}), 0.0, 1.0, pluss::make_series({1.0}), pluss::make_series({1.0}), options);
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

